Given a string s, find the longest palindromic subsequence's length in s.
A subsequence is a sequence that can be derived from another sequence by deleting some or no elements without changing
the order of the remaining elements.

Example 1:
Input: s = "bbbab"
Output: 4
Explanation: One possible longest palindromic subsequence is "bbbb".

Example 2:
Input: s = "cbbd"
Output: 2
Explanation: One possible longest palindromic subsequence is "bb".

Constraints:
1 <= s.length <= 1000
s consists only of lowercase English letters.

Approach:
The solution considered here is the interval approach where we use two indexes start and end. Here start = 0 and end =
n-1. There are only two possibilities here match or do not match. If the characters at these indexes match then we add
2 here and add the remaining ans from the recursion which moves both the indexes in their respective directions. Else, if
the characters do not match then we take the max out of two recursive calls where we move one index and the other remains
as such. The base case is when the two indexes forming the interval cross each other or when they land on the same index
in which case the length to be considered will be 1. The tabulation has some minute optimisations which if done then will
increase efficiency.

Note:
1. Don't try the brute force approach where you procure each subsequence and check if it is a palindrome. This will
force you to generate each subsequence hence increase the time complexity which will be (2^n) x n. Also, this solution
will be difficult to convert into a dp solution.

2. This problem can also be solved by taking the LCS between the given string s and reverse(s) which will in turn give
the length of longest common subsequence.

Naive approach -> TC = O(2^n), SC = O(n) (recursion stack space)
Memoization -> TC = O(n^2), SC = O(n^2) + O(n) (recursion stack space) = O(n^2)
Tabulation -> TC = O(n^2), SC = O(n^2)
Tabulation_SpaceOptimisation_1 -> TC = O(n^2), SC = O(2 * n) = O(n)
Tabulation_SpaceOptimisation_2 -> TC = O(n^2), SC = O(n)